### What is an offset commit in Kafka?

An offset is Kafka's record of how far a consumer group has consumed each partition. Committing offset N means you have safely processed everything up to N. If you crash, you restart from the last committed offset.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

/**
 * Conceptual: offset is the bookmark.
 */
public class OffsetConceptDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Partition 0 has records at offsets 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5
        // Consumer processed up to offset 3 and committed 3
        // If consumer crashes, it restarts from offset 3
        System.out.println("Last committed offset: 3");
        System.out.println("On restart, consumer reads from offset 3 onward");
    }
}
```

```text
Last committed offset: 3
On restart, consumer reads from offset 3 onward
```

### What is BATCH AckMode (the default)?

BATCH waits until every record returned by one `poll()` has been processed and then commits the highest offset in one go. It is efficient but a failure midway through a batch can replay earlier records.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class BatchAckListener {
    // Default AckMode is BATCH: commit after entire poll batch
    @KafkaListener(topics = "settlements", groupId = "settlement-group")
    public void onSettlement(String event) {
        System.out.println("Processing: " + event);
        // Container commits after ALL records from this poll are done
    }
}
```

```text
Processing: {"account":"SAV2001","amount":50000}
Processing: {"account":"SAV3001","amount":25000}
# Offset committed once after both records processed
```

### What is RECORD AckMode?

RECORD commits after each individual record. The redelivery window is tighter than BATCH but commit traffic is higher.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.kafka.config.ConcurrentKafkaListenerContainerFactory;
import org.springframework.kafka.listener.ContainerProperties;

@Configuration
public class RecordAckConfig {
    @Bean
    public ConcurrentKafkaListenerContainerFactory<String, String> recordAckFactory() {
        ConcurrentKafkaListenerContainerFactory<String, String> factory =
            new ConcurrentKafkaListenerContainerFactory<>();
        factory.getContainerProperties().setAckMode(ContainerProperties.AckMode.RECORD);
        return factory;
    }
}
```

```text
# Record 1 processed -> offset 1 committed
# Record 2 processed -> offset 2 committed
# Crash after record 2 committed: restart from offset 3, no replay
```

### What is MANUAL acknowledgment?

MANUAL and MANUAL_IMMEDIATE put you in charge. Spring injects an `Acknowledgment` into your listener and you decide when to commit. This is critical when the commit must be tied to a side effect succeeding.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.kafka.support.Acknowledgment;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class ManualAckListener {

    @KafkaListener(topics = "settlements", groupId = "settlement-group",
        containerFactory = "manualAckFactory")
    public void onSettlement(String event, Acknowledgment ack) {
        System.out.println("Processing: " + event);
        // Only acknowledge after the DB write succeeds
        settlementRepository.save(event);
        ack.acknowledge();
        System.out.println("Acknowledged after DB write");
    }

    // If DB throws, we do NOT acknowledge -> Kafka redelivers
}
```

```text
Processing: {"account":"SAV2001","amount":50000}
Acknowledged after DB write
```

### Why does commit-after-processing give at-least-once?

The container calls your method, your method does the work, and only then does the offset advance. If the process dies at any point before the commit lands, the broker still has the old offset. On restart, the consumer re-reads from that old offset and processes the records again. Records are never lost but may be delivered more than once.

```text
Scenario: crash between processing and commit

1. Consumer reads offset 5: {account: SAV2001, amount: 50000}
2. Listener processes: DB write succeeds
3. CRASH before offset 5 is committed
4. Restart: consumer reads from last committed offset (4)
5. Offset 5 is redelivered and processed AGAIN

Result: SAV2001 settlement processed twice -- at-least-once
Fix: make processing idempotent (primary key = txnId)
```

### How do you make processing idempotent to handle redelivery?

At-least-once plus idempotent processing gives effectively-once. Use a unique key like transaction ID as the primary key in the database so a duplicate write is a harmless no-op.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class IdempotentSettlementService {
    public void settle(String txnId, String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (txn_id) DO NOTHING
        // If this txnId was already settled, the insert is a no-op
        System.out.println("Settling txn=" + txnId + " account=" + accountId
            + " amount=" + amount);
        System.out.println("Idempotent: duplicate insert is a harmless no-op");
    }
}
```

```text
Settling txn=TXN-12345 account=SAV2001 amount=50000
Idempotent: duplicate insert is a harmless no-op
```

### What is the difference between commit-before vs commit-after?

| Timing | Guarantee | Risk |
|---|---|---|
| Commit before processing | At-most-once | Crash after commit loses the record |
| Commit after processing | At-least-once | Crash before commit causes redelivery |
| Kafka transactions | Exactly-once (within Kafka) | Heavier, needs transactional producer |

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class CommitTimingDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // At-most-once: commit first, then process
        System.out.println("At-most-once: offset committed -> process -> crash = record LOST");

        // At-least-once: process first, then commit
        System.out.println("At-least-once: process -> offset committed -> crash before commit = REDELIVERY");

        // Spring Kafka default (BATCH) is at-least-once
    }
}
```

```text
At-most-once: offset committed -> process -> crash = record LOST
At-least-once: process -> offset committed -> crash before commit = REDELIVERY
```

### When should you use MANUAL vs BATCH?

Use MANUAL when the commit must be tied to a side effect: only acknowledge after the DB write and the downstream publish both succeed. Use BATCH (default) for simpler cases where the listener method itself is the complete unit of work.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.kafka.support.Acknowledgment;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class ComplexAckListener {

    @KafkaListener(topics = "transfers", groupId = "transfer-group",
        containerFactory = "manualAckFactory")
    public void onTransfer(String event, Acknowledgment ack) {
        try {
            transferRepository.save(event);     // DB write
            notificationService.notify(event);  // downstream call
            ack.acknowledge();                  // commit only after BOTH succeed
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // Do NOT acknowledge -- Kafka will redeliver
            System.err.println("Processing failed, will be redelivered: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
# Success path: DB write + notify + acknowledge -> offset advances
# Failure path: exception -> no acknowledge -> offset stays -> redelivery
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Changing AckMode only shrinks or widens the replay window, it never eliminates replays. RECORD gives the tightest window; BATCH is efficient; MANUAL gives explicit control. At-least-once is the realistic guarantee for the consume-and-act side. The way to make it safe is idempotent processing, using a dedup key so a replay is harmless.

```bash
# Summary of AckModes
# BATCH (default): commit after poll batch -- efficient, larger replay window
# RECORD:          commit per record -- tighter replay, more commit traffic
# TIME:            commit on interval -- throughput-oriented
# COUNT:           commit after N records -- throughput-oriented
# MANUAL:          you call ack.acknowledge() -- commit queued to batch end
# MANUAL_IMMEDIATE: you call ack.acknowledge() -- commit right now
```
